@format tqs 1.0

!#
  Northwind rule canon — the derived half of the standard QQL test corpus.

  These three rules are not a demo. They are chosen so that the rule closure
  can DETECT AN ORDERING BUG, which a set of independent rules cannot: each
  reads what the one before it derived, so a closure computed in the wrong
  order is a different closure, not merely a slower one.

    LineRevenue        computes from base facts only
    StrandedLine       reads LineRevenue's output (positive chain)
    CleanCatalogEntry  negates StrandedLine's output

  That is TWO strata, not three: positive chaining converges by fixpoint
  WITHIN a stratum, so LineRevenue and StrandedLine share one. Only the
  negation forces a stratum boundary, because FILTER NOT EXISTS over a derived
  predicate cannot be evaluated until that predicate is complete.

  CleanCatalogEntry is the load-bearing one. FILTER NOT EXISTS over a DERIVED
  predicate is sound only if that predicate is fully materialized first, so it
  is the case that actually tests the stratification claim instead of assuming
  it. Run it before StrandedLine finishes and 8 discontinued products are
  wrongly declared clean — a wrong answer that looks entirely well-formed.

  Expected yield on the unmodified corpus, asserted by the determinism gate:
    :lineRevenue        2155   (one per order-detail line)
    :hasStrandedLine     228   (lines on the 8 discontinued products)
    :cleanCatalogEntry    69   (77 products minus those 8)

  A negation must not be vacuous or it proves nothing: every product appears
  on an order detail and every order carries lines, so "never sold" and
  "order with no lines" both derive zero. `:discontinued` splits 8/69, which
  is why the chain routes through it.
#!

@rules "NorthwindCanon" {

    # Revenue is not stored. It is business semantics — specifically the
    # decision that revenue is net of discount — and belongs in a rule rather
    # than being re-derived in every query that asks for it.
    "LineRevenue" :
    WHEN  ?d :unitPrice ?up .
          ?d :quantity ?qt .
          ?d :discount ?dc .
          BIND(?up * ?qt * (1 - ?dc) AS ?rev)
    THEN  ?d :lineRevenue ?rev .

    # Positive chaining: reads :lineRevenue, which no base fact carries.
    # Exercises the within-stratum fixpoint — if rule evaluation did not
    # iterate, this derives nothing on the pass where LineRevenue first fires.
    "StrandedLine" :
    WHEN  ?d :lineRevenue ?rev .
          ?d :hasProduct ?p .
          ?p :discontinued true .
    THEN  ?p :hasStrandedLine ?d .

    # Stratified negation over a derived predicate.
    "CleanCatalogEntry" :
    WHEN  ?p rdf:type :Product .
          FILTER NOT EXISTS { ?p :hasStrandedLine ?line }
    THEN  ?p :cleanCatalogEntry true .
}
